<div class="draftbar">Value Chains — a research layer of the Critical Materials Atlas</div>
<header class="topbar"><div class="wrap"><a class="wordmark" href="../"><span class="mark"></span>Critical Materials Atlas</a><nav class="topnav"><a href="../explorer">Explore</a><a href="../value-chains">Value chains</a><a href="../analysis">Research</a><a href="../method">Method</a><a href="../data">Download data</a></nav></div></header>
<main id="main">
<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Hydrogen · Materials and electrolysis</div>
  <h1>$title</h1>
  <p class="deck">$deck</p>
  <p class="byline">Evidence review · <time datetime="$date">25 November 2026</time> · <a href="PRODUCTION.md">Claim register</a></p>
</div></section>
<article>
  <p>Proton-exchange membrane (PEM) electrolysers usually use an iridium-based anode catalyst. The amount needed each year depends on how much new PEM capacity is built, how much iridium each stack uses and how many stacks are replaced. Alkaline, anion-exchange membrane and solid-oxide systems offer different routes for suitable applications. This study compares those choices with an explicit annual iridium budget.</p>

  <h2>The installed base and the annual market</h2>
  <p>Global installed electrolysis capacity exceeded $capacity_stock GW in 2025 across technologies. The IEA identified more than $construction GW under construction targeting operation in 2026. Both refer to rated electrical input capacity. The model below uses annual additions, with a separate PEM share. The IEA identifies costs, market demand and project delays as important in the current market. $deployment.</p>
  <p>Iridium is recovered as a minor by-product of platinum-group metal mining, concentrated in South Africa. Production decisions respond to the economics of the whole mix of metals. The study uses the published $reference_supply tonnes per year as a primary-output reference. Johnson Matthey separately estimates $market_primary tonnes of primary supply in 2025 on a producer-sales basis, against $market_demand tonnes of net demand. Its $electrochemical_demand tonnes of electrochemical demand cover several uses. The amount available to PEM manufacturers depends on allocation across those uses, stocks and contracts. $study. $pgms. $market. $market_basis.</p>
  <table class="numbers">
    <caption><strong>Industry estimates.</strong> Global iridium demand by category in 2025, tonnes net of closed-loop returns and sales of surplus metal</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Tonnes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
$market_rows
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Mining, refining and catalyst-coated membrane production can occur in different places. Johnson Matthey reports separating iridium and other platinum-group metals at its Royston refinery in the United Kingdom and describes UK manufacturing operations for electrolyser components. The company disclosures illustrate these downstream stages. Country shares of qualified refinery or catalyst capacity remain unmeasured here. $refinery. $components.</p>

  <h2>Stack choice changes material exposure</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Electrolyser technology comparison">
    <table>
      <caption>Representative stack designs and operating considerations</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Technology</th><th scope="col">Representative materials</th><th scope="col">Operating and substitution considerations</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">PEM</th><td>Iridium-based anode, platinum at the cathode, titanium components</td><td>High current density and flexible operation. Lower loadings must also meet performance and durability requirements.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Alkaline</th><td>Nickel-based electrodes and catalysts in conventional designs</td><td>Commercial route. Minimum load, response and pressure depend on the system and operating conditions.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Anion-exchange membrane (AEM)</th><td>Research and products use a range of catalysts, including nickel-based designs</td><td>Membrane and stack durability, project scale and vendor availability matter.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Solid oxide (SOEC)</th><td>Ceramic electrolytes and nickel-containing electrodes in common designs</td><td>Uses high-temperature steam and a suitable heat source. Thermal cycling can affect durability.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The material comparison follows representative designs. A particular project would also compare electricity and heat requirements, operating flexibility, lifetime, cost and hydrogen delivery conditions. $irena. $doe.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Iridium loading evidence">
    <table>
      <caption>Published intensities by evidence type, in kg of elemental iridium per MW of electrical stack rating</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">kg Ir/MW</th><th scope="col">Evidence type</th><th scope="col">Basis and limits</th></tr></thead><tbody>
$intensity_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>To convert an elemental iridium loading in mg per cm², divide by the electrical power density in W per cm² at the same operating point. The result in mg per W equals kg per MW. An iridium oxide mass needs conversion to elemental iridium first. DOE’s targets for total platinum-group metals include platinum and use a different numerator. $doe.</p>

  <h2>How much new PEM fits within an annual iridium budget?</h2>
  <p><strong>Illustrative calculation.</strong> Annual new PEM capacity in GW multiplied by kg Ir per MW gives annual tonnes of iridium. Dividing an assumed allocation of the $reference_supply tonnes of primary output by the intensity gives the annual new PEM capacity at equality. This first calculation covers elemental iridium in new stacks alone, with full manufacturing yield. Loading and allocation are scenario choices.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Annual PEM capacity at equality">
    <table class="numbers">
      <caption><strong>Modelled.</strong> Annual new PEM capacity at equality, in GW per year, by assumed share of global primary output</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">kg Ir per MW</th>$share_columns</tr></thead><tbody>
$threshold_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" id="frontier" data-primary="$reference_supply">
    <form id="frontier-controls" hidden>
      <label for="pem-additions">Annual new PEM capacity (GW/year)<input type="range" id="pem-additions" min="1" max="100" value="$gw_default"><output id="pem-gw" for="pem-additions">$gw_default GW/year</output></label>
      <label for="ir-loading">Iridium intensity (kg/MW)<select id="ir-loading">$intensity_options</select></label>
      <label for="ir-share">Share of primary output allocated to PEM<select id="ir-share">$share_options</select></label>
    </form>
    <div id="frontier-results" aria-live="polite">
      <span>Gross requirement <strong id="ir-gross">$gross t/year</strong></span>
      <span>Assumed primary supply <strong id="ir-available">$available t/year</strong></span>
      <span>Illustrative gap <strong id="ir-gap">$gap t/year</strong></span>
      <span>Annual additions at equality <strong id="ir-threshold">$equality GW/year</strong></span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The initial example uses $gw_default GW of new PEM per year, $example_loading kg Ir per MW and $example_share of world primary output. At those additions and that allocation, equality requires $loading_at_equality kg Ir per MW. The <a href="out/illustrative_scenario_matrix.csv">scenario matrix (CSV)</a> and <a href="out/illustrative_thresholds.csv">equality thresholds (CSV)</a> reproduce the one-year examples.</p>

  <h2>Replacement and recycling change the annual balance</h2>
  <p><strong>Modelled.</strong> The three paths are illustrative schedules for new electrolysis across all technologies from $start to $end. Each starts with an empty modelled fleet. The reference assigns $ref_share of new capacity to PEM, keeps loading at $ref_intensity kg Ir per MW and allocates $primary_share of the primary reference to PEM ($primary_allocation tonnes per year). That loading is below the 0.34 to 2.0 kg Ir per MW that a 2026 review reports for 2024, so the reference case is optimistic about catalyst loading. The model follows scheduled capacity even in years with a material gap. Later recycling is therefore conditional on those earlier installations going ahead.</p>
  <figure>
    <figcaption><strong>Modelled.</strong> Annual primary iridium required after delayed recovery, in tonnes per year. All panels run from zero to $ceiling. The dashed line shows the assumed $primary_allocation tonnes per year available to PEM.</figcaption>
    <div class="annual-charts" aria-hidden="true">
$charts
    </div>
  </figure>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Annual iridium demand and supply">
    <table class="numbers">
      <caption>Selected years from the plotted series, in tonnes per year</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Primary allocation</th>$annual_headers</tr></thead><tbody>
$yearly_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The operating-life reference is $operating_hours hours from the IEA assumptions annex. At the model’s $utilisation utilisation it rounds up to $calendar_stack calendar years between stack changes. Stacks are replaced while plants remain in service. The model assumes a $plant_years-year plant life, $manufacturing_yield manufacturing yield, $recovery recovery of old stack iridium after collection and processing, and a $lag-year delay before material returns. Recovered metal enters the supply balance only after it returns. $annex. $study.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Timing of first modelled gap">
    <table>
      <caption>First year in which the annual requirement exceeds the assumed allocation</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Path</th><th scope="col">First modelled gap</th></tr></thead><tbody>
$first_gaps
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Reducing loading, replacing PEM and recovering metal</h2>
  <p>These cases share the middle additions path. The lower-loading case reaches 0.05 kg Ir per MW in 2035. The substitution case assigns 25% of new capacity to PEM. Each case reruns the stack and recycling balance, so savings overlap. Lower loading also reduces how much iridium will return from those stacks later. A paired case halves assumed stack hours alongside lower loading to test the effect of more frequent replacement.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Mitigation comparison under the middle path">
    <table class="numbers">
      <caption><strong>Modelled.</strong> Cumulative requirement and sum of annual gaps over the study period, in tonnes</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Case</th><th scope="col">Primary iridium required</th><th scope="col">Sum of annual gaps</th><th scope="col">First gap year</th></tr></thead><tbody>
$mitigation_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Using the 2025 producer-sales estimate as the primary reference, at the same assumed allocation, moves the reference sum of gaps from $cumulative_ref_gap to $cumulative_market_gap tonnes. Across the full sensitivity set, which varies the supply share, utilisation, recovery and the return delay in the middle path with reference loading, the sum ranges from $low_sensitivity to $high_sensitivity tonnes. The gap calculation carries forward only recovered metal left over from earlier years. Existing inventories, primary expansion, refurbishment and price or procurement responses sit outside this illustration.</p>
  <p>Published research already models iridium constraints, replacement and recycling, including feasible rates of PEM growth. This study contributes a small reproducible set of annual thresholds and tests how explicit technology and recovery choices change the balance. Estimating future shortages would require calibrated project schedules, qualified recycling volumes, stack specifications and competing demand. $study. $targets.</p>
  <p>Download the <a href="out/illustrative_annual_flows.csv">annual flows</a>, <a href="out/illustrative_mitigation.csv">mitigation cases</a> and <a href="out/illustrative_sensitivity.csv">sensitivity results</a> as CSV files.</p>

  <h2>Customs trade appendix</h2>
  <p>The frozen BACI series measures nominal trade in mixed-use categories. HS 854330 includes electroplating, electrolysis and electrophoresis equipment. HS 711049 combines semi-manufactured iridium, osmium and ruthenium. The series cannot isolate PEM equipment, iridium flows or the geography of catalyst manufacturing. $machinery. $metals. $baci.</p>
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="control" id="static-trade" hidden><label for="trade-slider">Trade year</label><input id="trade-slider" type="range" min="0" max="0" value="0"></div>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Trade in the two customs categories">
      <table>
        <caption><strong>Reported customs trade.</strong> BACI V202601 · <span id="trade-year" aria-live="polite">$trade_year</span></caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">World export value</th><th scope="col">Leading exporter</th><th scope="col">Coverage</th></tr></thead><tbody id="trade-body">
$trade_rows
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Evidence and reproduction</h2>
  <p>See the <a href="PRODUCTION.md">claim audit and literature comparison</a>, <a href="README.md">methods and commands</a>, <a href="evidence_inputs.json">source and scenario inputs</a>, <a href="out/electrolyser_chain.json">calculated evidence</a> and <a href="out/electrolyser_trade.json">customs extract</a>.</p>
</article>
